    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Shortly after Vargas began leasing the land from the county, he was joined by Christina Juarez, who's from Tijuana. The farm, like the surrounding area, is bilingual. Together, they realized that a workers' cooperative was the best way forward for the farm. She said, I believe you could do work with more heart, when you feel equal to the other person, when you don't expect orders from them, when you feel like they won't scold you because something is different, and so you're putting your heart and your soul and your knowledge into something. But it hasn't been easy. With four worker owners, they're just beginning to pay themselves minimum wage. And nature hasn't exactly been cooperating. When the Tijuana River Valley floods, all the produce it touches has to be thrown out.",
